        "kind": "observation",
        "laterStatus": "not_applicable",
        "notes": "Worsley took departure from Wild Camp at 12.30pm on 24 April 1916 using 61d 4' S, 54d 50' W for Cape Belsham - a position read off the chartlet in Nordenskjold and Andersson (1905: 77), which took it from Fricker (1898) and in turn from Stieler's Hand-Atlas of 1891, at 1:8,000,000. The boat journey therefore starts from an inherited cartographic position rather than an observed one, which is why this row is filed as inherited_cartography and not as a fix. A time sight taken at the camp that morning to rate the chronometer gave 54d 19' 45\" W, and Worsley did not adopt it: the log says the longitude of the cape was only approximately known to them. Bergman and others find the assumed position within 2.25 nautical miles of the modern one.",
